                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rancid, sour or musty smell</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fat oxidation, moisture or mold</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Toss</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Flat, stale or cardboard taste</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flavor loss with age</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usable, but replace soon</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bugs or webbing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pantry pests</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Toss</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="by-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Shelf Life by Type: Whey, Plant, Collagen and Gainers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not every powder ages at the same speed. Four things set the pace: how much lactose or other sugar is present to feed browning, how much fat is present to go rancid, whether the formula leans on vitamins, probiotics or enzymes that lose potency over time, and how readily the powder pulls moisture out of the air. A plain isolate and a fortified mass gainer sitting side by side in the same cupboard will not age at the same rate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whey concentrate, usually 70 to 80% protein by weight, keeps more lactose and milk fat than isolate, so it has more raw material for browning and staleness. Whey isolate, typically 90% protein or more, has very little of either and tends to age a little more gracefully, although the 2009 study still found consumers noticed stored isolate by 18 months. If you only use a scoop a few times a week, a <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">whey protein isolate</a> is the better bet for a tub that will sit open for a while. Casein behaves much like whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plant blends of pea, rice, soy, hemp or pumpkin seed contain no lactose, so they avoid the classic whey browning, but they bring their own issues. Seed-based proteins such as hemp and pumpkin carry plant oils that can oxidize, and many plant powders soak up humidity readily. An earthy or grassy smell is normal for these; a sour or paint-like one is not. Most of the <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">plant-based protein powders</a> we track print best-by dates around two years out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Collagen peptides are about the most stable protein powder you can buy. They are nearly pure protein with essentially no lactose and very little fat, and many collagen brands print best-by dates two to three years from manufacture. Their weak point is moisture: hydrolyzed collagen is very hygroscopic and can set into a solid brick in a humid kitchen. Keep the lid tight and the scoop dry. Our <a href="/best-collagen-protein-powder/">collagen protein powder guide</a> covers which products are worth buying in the first place.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mass gainers and meal replacement powders are the most perishable of the group. They pair protein with large amounts of carbohydrate, added fats such as MCT oil or nut butter powder, and often a full vitamin and mineral premix. That means more ingredients that can go stale or rancid, and vitamin levels that may drift below the label claim past the date. The same applies to powders sold on probiotics or digestive enzymes, where the date matters more. Buy a <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-weight-gain/">weight-gain powder</a> in a size you will finish quickly.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
