        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 md:p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Protein powder does expire, but slowly and mostly in taste rather than safety. The best-by date is the manufacturer's quality estimate, and the whey storage studies show sealed powder holding up for 12 to 18 months or more at normal temperatures, with heat the main thing that shortens that.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Keep tubs sealed, cool and dry, use a dry scoop, skip the fridge, and buy a size you will finish within a few months of opening. If a tub smells off, has hard wet clumps or shows mold, throw it out. If it is just a little stale, bake with it and buy a smaller tub next time.
          </p>
